Language name: Aether - Logan Sourile

In this lab, we added the variables (declaration, Access, and Assignment) and scope 
to the language. 
To add these functional aspects, I created several new classes. I added the Variable 
class (to help with IDENTIFIERS in the literal parsing), a varDecl (for variable
declaration AST nodes), a varAssign(for variable assignment AST nodes), an 
Environment class (for variable storage), and a block class (for the creation 
of scopes

Some design choices I made in my language included allowing programmers to create 
a variable without an initializer, but if they tried to access it, it would throw 
an error saying it wasn't initialized yet. This helps reduce issues related to 
null/nil values being in the program, and if they are caught as errors 
and instead initialized beforehand, we can make sure clean programming 
is written. 

Another design choice I made was to throw a runtime error if the user tries to
access a variable before they declare it. This was made this way to also ensure 
we do not have messy declarations and keep everything streamlined. To access a 
variable, it needs to be made first. 

One other design choice I made with the variables was to not allow redeclaration
in my language. I disallow users from putting: var a = 1; var a = 2; and instead
this creates an error. I wanted to make sure that the code that is being written 
is easy to read, and if you have multiple variable names being redeclared all the 
time, it can get confusing about what is going on. 

The scope aspect of this lab uses part of the environment class and the block 
class to handle scope. The environment class is used to hold all the variables for
a certain scope. So each time a new block is made, the environment class keeps track
of that environment's predecessor. 

The block part in my parser goes through the different declarations/statements in 
the block and adds these to my blocks list of declarations. It parses through all 
these declarations and adds any of the variables that it comes across into its own
environment/scope. 

In my evaluator, we take any variable declarations and run the define method in 
its designated environment we are in. If we run into a variable token, we need to 
access it, so we use the "get" method in the designated environment we are in. If
we cannot find the variable IDENTIFIER, we recursively look through each predecessor
environment to find it until we hit the global environment. Lastly, we have the 
assignment part of the evaluation, where we have to check if the variable exists 
(if not, check parent environments), and then whether we are looking to assign the 
variable to something or if we have an expression starting with an IDENTIFIER. 

Lastly, we added the error handling for missing braces in blocks and invalid assignments
in the variable assignment portion of the variable additions. I also updated the 
synchronise method to also detect if we hit a new block or if we found a new variable
declaration. 

